ilization issues and  many of our really good reference labs  do a nice job of trying to bring those  things to our attention even though it  might drive work away from them they're  trying to provide us a service and help  us address test utilization another way  to look at this is we can hopefully  start to look at what test should we be  doing in-house ourselves versus  continuing to send out to a reference  lab I have these this last slide in this  slide slightly out of order because this  again goes back to look at benchmarking  and again what you see is this is  measuring bill tests in a variety of  ways so bill tests / cute discharge  build test for acute patient a acute as  our lab test versus discharge adjusted  discharge for complexity or adjusted  patient day based on complexity and  again looking at where you are we are  lab X what is our percentile rank again  a lower number is better what is the  25th percentile so we kind of seed 25th  and where we might have the opportunity  to move to so for example of a look at  the second line inpatient build test per  patient day we are at the 40th  percentile but the 25th percentiles at  seven we're way off on a patient day  perspective and so again how you look at  your data how you pick your targets is  very very important so just using  impatient test utilization because  that's what that last slide was about  and when I was at my previous position  that is where we started to look at  inpatient utilization and for us at the  University of Minnesota impatient  utilization was about half of all the  testing done in the laboratories on that  campus so you need to kind of start with  that broad high-level look  at your utilization and one strategy  again there's many many strategies here  but is to look at it on a DRG basis  which dr g's and we did it in other jobs  have done similar things with blood  management which dr g's are the biggest  consumers of your product whether it be  lab testing whether it be blood products  and really trying to kind of narrow down  your focus so you're not trying to boil  the ocean so to speak so look at all lab  testing by dr g and then you can dig  deeper into that maybe you've identified  your top 20 drgs and start to look at it  by service by physician you can dig even  deeper and look at it by specific tests  and you can dig even deeper depending on  your data sources by what are the  outcomes what is the length of stay what  is the cost again you you need to start  at the top at a high level look and then  keep digging deeper to help help you  define your strategies this is a very  hard slide to read but it's looking at  that saying what are the top ms-drgs in  my system and in this case 20 ERG  category is accounted for forty three  percent of all test utilization in this  institution so this is a way to start  because you can address a significant  amount of your utilization by focusing  in on these DRG categories and then you  can start to look and do some comparison  again it just depends on your data  sources this happens to be from the UHC  University Health consortium but in  various areas I could look at how I  compared in the upper left-hand corner  for example heart transplant lower  left-hand corner liver transplant and  then various categories of drgs and  where was I looking at tests per case  versus where was somebody else again you  have that opportunity to compare to a  group you can compare to specific people  in the group but trying to get that let  that high level look at where do we  pair with the people we want to be  compared to moving on a bit one of the  key aspects for utilization management  is to really have a structure an ongoing  project or process around utilization  management and a key piece of that  becomes the utilization management  committee with goals to you know to look  at optimizing your utilization for your  patience reducing unnecessary testing  and therefore reducing costs trying to  get best practice and so again depending  on where you are and what sources of  data and what benchmarking you have you  know set some best practice targets for  yourself improve appropriateness by  using evidence-based best practice  protocols provide consultative guidance  to the physicians and the residents to  help them order the right tests we'll  talk about some aspects of this as we go  through this when you form the  utilization management committee you  want to make sure you have the right  people at the table a bias of mine and  if you look in the literature this tends  to be true it's not always successful if  it's if it's a top-down administration  downward type of thing or even if it's a  lab to physician type of thing it needs  to be collaborative and it's usually  more successful if the physicians or the  providers themselves are helping to lead  lead that process and then the lab can  facilitate that process make sure you  have high level leadership support your  CEO your chief operating officer your  chief financial officer whatever it be  it's important for this to be an  important priority of this of your  organization make sure your process is  open and transparent and in that it's  multidisciplinary its key key key HS  emphasize it enough that information  technology is key to the success of this  there's an opportunity to use tools that  help your process so you need active  support participation in partnership  focus on patient care does practice cost  reduction will happen but don't make  that be the focus of why you're starting  and where  you're going that will happen if you do  this properly and then you have to  remember to monitor and report and that  helps build your credibility it helps  build support for the next thing you  want to tackle and to make sure you  share your successes and celebrate them  so examples these are just examples  again they come from my previous  position because I'm relatively new in  this position we developed alerts in our  epic electronic medical record system to  help us address duplicate testing and I  keep in touch with the people there and  I know that just recently they've  implemented one of these develop we  developed a partnership with our  reference lab so that's one thing i'd  always recommend and look at what  resources are available to you to help  you because there are people out there  who've done some of this and whether  it's through networking that you talk to  other people or you use organizations  that you already have relationships with  it can be very helpful and for fairview  a RUP was going to help them develop a  formulary add genetic counselors support  to help you review all those expensive  molecular and genetic test orders so  this was in the process of occurring  right as i left fairview but as I'm now  in Health Partners and park nicollet we  have recently hired two genetic  counselors that will be based in the  laboratory one of them started two weeks  ago and the other one starts next Monday  and i'll be talking about genetic  testing at the end but if this this in  itself is a key component to our  strategy we work with our benchmarking  are our performance data analysis  program in the case of fairview that at  the university that was University  Health consortium we worked with the  residency program and actually they  reached out to us they wanted to make  their residents more aware of pricing  and cost when they ordered tests and if  anybody ever comes and asks you that  don't please don't avoid that question  and ignore it because it's a great  opportunity communication is important  and then ultimately developing sometimes  developing physician report cards  he'll compare performance laboratory  tests formulary which is one component  we're all familiar with formularies in  the pharmacy world it's basically a  utilization device where you can help  focus on things that you allow to be  ordered usually things that you make  more difficult to order things that you  don't allow to be ordered and it's been  done for years and years in pharmacy and  now it's becoming more and more common  in the laboratory we want to make it  easier for clinicians to order the right  tests and hard for them to order the  wrong tests we usually do it on a  criteria based or a tier sort of  structure if a test really only needs to  be ordered by a small group of specific  physicians then you restrict it to that  small group of physicians and don't  allow other people to order those tests  and an example of a tiered process could  be a test that anybody can order a test  that only specific specialists can order  more tests that maybe require further  approval before they can be ordered by  anybody and that could be a genetic  counselor that proves that a department  head proves that a pathologist approves  it testing algorithms are another tool  in the toolkit of test utilization these  are all about best practice and  evidence-based testing protocols usually  they're disease-specific there are tons  and tons of examples of these in the  literature whether they're from mayo or  a RUP or academic health centers people  all over the country have implemented  these types of protocols and you don't  need to create them from scratch there's  different types of algorithms there's  some of them RIT driven where there's  clinical input and information but then  you are able to program it and have it  the function based on the IT system some  are laboratory driven apologists driven  genetic counselor driven there can be  different types of algorithms that you  can use to look at testing so in a  variety of organizations I've worked for  duplicate testing  to be a significant issue that  contributes to over use of testing and  inappropriate use and a waste of money  sometimes it's due to the the misuse of  your electronic medical record and the  misuse of physician order entry there's  functionality that exists in those  systems but physicians don't always use  them I'm finding just in the  organization I mean with now it's more  in a problem in one half of organization  but they found a way in the other half  of the organization to make those tools  more they force them a little bit more  so that they're used properly when you  have multiple positions on the same case  that can often result in duplicate  testing overuse and abuse of standing  orders and the fact that standing orders  can stay open forever can sometimes lead  to overuse of lab testing defensive  medicine we don't want to be sued we  want to make sure we cover all our bases  we order everything when you have  residents in an academic setting that  can often or result in over ordering or  just the fact that your test catalogs  and the names of your tests and the  descriptions are confusing can lead to a  fair amount of miss utilization or  inappropriate some strategies for this  you can build rules in your electronic  medical record we use the phrases soft  stop and hard stop a soft stop is just  when something pops up and tells you  something it hopes you do the right  thing a hard stop makes you do the right  thing it doesn't allow you to proceed if  you've done something that you're not  supposed to education is part of it but  it can't be the only piece limiting the  time frames on your standing orders  cleaning up your test catalog and then  reviewing the physician and specialists  favorite favorite lists in your  electronic medical record we find that  you know your epic system or whatever  your electronic medical record gets set  up and then you're up and live on it for  years and years and you never go back  and take a fresh look at those those  frequent ordered lists or those favorite  lists and a lot of times the misuse  because those have not been adjusted  this talks about that soft stop vs hard  stop if you want a good resource there's  a lot in the literature from the  Cleveland Clinic has been a lot of work  in this regard and they did a lot of  studies and found that there was not a  lot of value when you just did a soft  stopped because it didn't force you to  do anything and if anything it just  annoyed doctors because all these things  popped up so you got this pop-up fatigue  and they just ignore it they don't read  it they bypass it it really they did  this they tried it it ultimately showed  them that they needed something firmer  and like I said in my previous  organization this soft stop technology  has recently been put in place for some  duplicate testing and it'll I'll be  interested to watch them and see if it's  successful or not this is very hard to  read it i guess it's harder to read than  i thought it was going to be but this is  a slide in this example of what  Cleveland Clinic did because they  ultimately did a hard stop and the  success of their hard stop was they  looked up testing and they started with  testing and they started with a short  list of tests that should never be  ordered more than once in a day or once  in 24 hours on an impatient ultimately  that got expanded to more than once in a  stay and maybe more than once in your  life because there are certain tests  like genetic tests that no deep don't  need to be ordered more than once in  your life but when one of those tests is  ordered this pops up and it basically  tells you this has already been ordered  so if we're talking about once in 24  hours they tried to order the test  hemoglobin a1c in this case you find out  it was already ordered in the previous  24 hours but not only do we just tell  you that we give you the result and we  tell you who ordered it and we tell you  when so and usually then that just stops  the whole process but this is a hard  stop they literally can't continue  without making a phone call to somebody  and maybe there's some special cause or  special reason  but this just stops them they can't  proceed in this case now we wanted to do  this at fairview but we just got pushed  back from our IT department even though  the physicians supported it we couldn't  get the IT department to do it now  Cleveland Clinic which is also an epic  they just did their own custom  programming that fear view they were not  not willing to do custom programming for  us hard stop you can do various  scenarios i've already talked about this  once in 24 hours once in an inpatient  stay once in a lifetime this sort of an  example of being the initial shortlist  that Cleveland Clinic did dr. Gary  Prokop is the person who has been very  very involved with this at the Cleveland  Clinic and has written about it and  talked about it so this was a short list  of tests that they decided to try as  their pilot ultimately they continued  with all these tests and ultimately  expanded beyond this list but they ended  up taking uric acid off because they  found there were certain patients  especially patients with certain  chemotherapy where it was appropriate to  do it more than once in 24 hours  duplicate testing alerts that's one kind  of what we just talked about other  places have done this example here  University of Mississippi Medical Center  it monitors things like CBC's and basic  metabolic panels it looks at tests that  are ordered duplicate within a four hour  period again they did a strategy they  put something in place and they saved  just on this strategy 146 thousand  dollars genetic testing so I told you to  talk about genetic testing specifically  it's a major effort and I'm deeply  involved with now in my new organization  and one of the things you need to  understand about the organization and in  now it's not only a provider of  healthcare and we have hospitals and  clinics but we are also a payer health  partners is one of the major players in  the state of Minnesota so we're able to  look at this both from what will we pay  or what will we approve and then also  from the clinical side about what what  we will do so genetic testing this