ve a unique  coding that goes with all of this which  makes the billing of it very complex but  it all leads to the cost and the  experience that we have with genetic  testing obviously this is all related to  the Triple Aim of evidence of  evidence-based quality care exceptional  experience for our patients and  affordability for our patient and I've  listed some other points here you know  affordability over in the lower  right-hand corner we talked a lot in our  organization about the total cost of  care and really pay a lot of attention  to that you know you can read these  bullet points yourself but all of this  does relate it to the Triple Aim and the  model that we're putting together if you  start at the top this is all about  knowledge management how do we manage  this ever-changing Dade daily change in  what the what the environment is in this  what the landscape is in this because  the testing keeps changing there's new  tests all the time so part of our  structure is we're going to be looking  for a software product to support us  where there's companies out there that  try to keep up with all of this so that  we don't have to try to keep up with all  of it ourselves we have we have a data  source that we can go to expert panels  so when we're considering whether we  should allow performance and payment for  different tests will put expert panels  of physicians from a specific specialty  together and we've decided both  physicians from our organization and  because we're a pair and pay we pay  multiple organizations we need to have  experts and physicians from other  organizations also and then developing a  genetic testing database then we kind of  split off into two things what do we do  on the care side the provider side and  what do we do on the plans side or the  payer side of this so on the care side  it's things like informed consent how do  we use epic as decision support how do  we use epic as ordering and process  support  we code and bill where do we send these  things to what are the labs we're going  to use on the planned side of the payer  side how do we provide information to  our members how do we how do we develop  coverage policies how do we deal with  prior notification and preauthorization  how do we added claims what are our  contracting and payment strategies so  this is the model that we are in the  beginning stages of in our organization  some of our current strategies are we've  developed a genetic testing committee  structure there's an Operations  Committee steering committee and kind of  a day-to-day lead committee I happen to  be on the lead committee which kind of  is driving some of this we have hired  two genetic counselors I've already  talked to you about that they work very  closely with our send out coordinators  that each site they review all genetic  test orders they determine the  appropriateness and approve modify or  delete orders communicate with  physicians review the medical record  they're really concentrating on on lab  test orders and right now they're  process is somewhat complicated due to  IT issues over time we will address  those they help us assure that informed  consent has happened and they  investigate the appropriate place to  send this testing to performing expert  panels already talked about that we have  started to develop two formulary  committees payer formulary committee and  then on the care side of provider  formulary committee we have entered into  a membership with the plugs program the  utilization program that started with  dr. my Caspian at Seattle Children's  Hospital and they do more than just  pediatrics they're a great resource for  us and then right now we're in the  process of evaluating these different  software systems and we have an RFP that  will be going out so that we can select  the appropriate tool for us to use back  to the genetic counselors our first  genetic counselor for the lab these are  people they don't do counseling with  